 ## Overview
-Visibility Culling is a family of acceleration techniques in image-order volume rendering that reduces computational load by identifying and excluding volume elements (voxels, chunks, or primitives) that do not contribute to the final image [S2][S4, S5]. It solves the problem of unnecessary sample evaluation and rasterization overhead for geometry that is occluded, outside the view, or visually insignificant [S2, S3, unofficial][S4]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is the diversity of culling criteria: while [Parallel Ray Casting with Segment Lists](2565-parallel-ray-casting-with-segment-lists.md) focuses on data structure traversal, and [SparseLeap](5152-sparseleap.md) on spatial skipping, Visibility Culling encompasses methods that operate on depth (occlusion), spatial extent (frustum), probabilistic approximation, or precomputed visibility zones [S1, S2, S3, S6, unofficial].
+Visibility Culling is a family of acceleration techniques in volume rendering that reduces computational load by identifying and excluding geometric primitives, voxels, or ray segments that do not contribute to the final image [S2, S3, unofficial][S4]. It solves the problem of unnecessary sample evaluations and rasterization overhead by performing conservative intersection tests between bounding volumes and the camera's viewing frustum or occlusion masks [S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Space Skipping and Empty Volume Culling](10063-space-skipping-and-empty-volume-culling.md), which focuses on spatial data structures to skip empty regions, this family focuses on view-dependent visibility to prune work units before or during the rendering pipeline [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
